## Supporting Information Placeholder
Zeolitic Imidazolate Frameworks (ZIFs) represent an emerging subclass of metal organic frameworks (MOFs) constructed from tetrahedral coordinated transition metal cations (M) bridged by imidazole-based ligands (Im). Because the M-Im-M angle in ZIF \((\sim\!145^{\circ})\) is similar to Si-O-Si angle in conventional silicon-based zeolites, ZIFs give rise to zeolite-type topology. Unlike zeolite, ZIFs are unique in structural flexibility including tunable framework, pore aperture, and surface area. Indeed, over 450 ZIF structures have to date been synthesized, many of which are microporous with inherently large surface areas and tunable cavities, and exhibit exceptional thermal and chemical stability. Thus, ZIFs demonstrate great promise in various applications such as gas separation and storage, chemical sensing, and heterogeneous catalysis.
Driven by the increasing global energy demand, recent development in the field has extended the application of ZIF materials toward photocatalysis using visible light. Although still in the early stage of exploration, ZIFs have been used as photocatalysts for dye and phenol degradation as well as \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction. In these systems, photoactive nanostructures/molecules are incorporated into the highly porous structure of ZIFs, where ZIFs are used as a simple host or passive medium for dispersing the catalytic active species. This strategy has been widely used in developing zeolite
based photocatalysts, and recently in preparing MOF/nanocomposite or MOF/molecular catalyst hybrids, yet suffers from challenges, notably the difficulty in preventing the guest material aggregation and the lack of the control over spatial distribution and homogeneity. An alternative approach, which has recently been used in developing photoactive MOFs, is to develop the framework which exhibits an intrinsic photochemical response and thereby can be directly applied in photocatalysis.
As photocatalysis is based on charge transfer (CT) event following photoexcitation, it is essential to gain an intimate knowledge of the excited state and charge transport properties within the ZIF framework, the properties that remain unexplored. In this work, we report the first study on the excited state dynamics in ZIF-67 thin film using optical transient (OTA) and X-ray transient absorption (XTA) spectroscopy. We show that charge separation (CS) occurs in the framework of ZIF-67 by forming a super-long intermediate state with ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) character.
ZIF-67 thin film was synthesized according to the established synthetic protocols.29 The details of the synthesis of ZIF-67 are described in supporting information (SI). The morphology of the ZIF-67 film was characterized by atomic force microscopy (AFM) in the PeakForce feedback mode. As shown in Figure 1a and b, the synthesized ZIF-67 film is continuous and consists of crystals with truncated rhombic dodecahedral shape with \(\sim\!300\!-\!500\ \mathrm{nm}\) size. The cross-section views of the film (Figure S1) indicates that the thickness of the film is \(\sim350\ \mathrm{nm}\), suggesting that the film is likely composed of single crystal layers.
The bulk structure of ZIF-67 was measured by powder X-ray diffraction (XRD). As shown in Figure 1c, XRD patterns obtained for the synthesized sample agrees well with the peaks characteristic of ZIF-67;30 suggesting the successful synthesis of ZIF-67. Furthermore, the local structure of ZIF-67 at Co center was examined using steady-state X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS). Figure 1d shows the X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) spectrum of ZIF-67 at Co K-edge. The XANES spectrum of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) which is the starting material known for octahedral \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{h}}\right)\) coordination at Co center, is also shown in Figure 1d for comparison. The prominent pre-edge feature (quadrupole allowed is-3d transition) in ZIF-67, characteristic of tetrahedral \(\left(\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{d}}\right)\) coordination,31,32 is distinct from the negligible is-3d transition in the \(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{h}}\) coordinated \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). Furthermore, the main absorption edge in the XANES spectrum of ZIF-67, arising from dipole allowed is-4p transition, shows a less sharp peak than that of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), which can be ascribed to the decreased multiple scattering for a \(\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{d}}\) coordination in the former compared with \(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{h}}\) geometry in the latter.33 The \(\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{d}}\) structure is further confirmed by quantitatively analyzing the extended X-ray absorption line structure (EXAFS) spectrum (inset of Figure 1d and Figure S2), which clearly demonstrates that Co has four nitrogen neighbors with the average distance of \(\mathrm{Co-N\,1.99\pm0.02\,\mathring{A}}\).
Figure 2a shows the UV-visible-near IR absorption spectrum of ZIF-67 film. The spectrum exhibits three absorption features, including a broad absorption in UV region \((<400\) nm), a visible band with three maxima centered at 588, 567, and \(539\ \mathrm{nm}\) (inset of Figure 2a), and a broad near IR band from 900-1400 nm. These features can be attributed to the LMCT transition, the higher-lying \(\left[^{4}\mathrm{A}_{2}(\mathrm{F})-^{4}\mathrm{T}_{1}(\mathrm{P})\right]\) and lower-lying \(\left[^{4}\mathrm{A}_{2}(\mathrm{F})-^{4}\mathrm{T}_{1}(\mathrm{F})\right]\) d-d ligand field transitions (Figure 2b), respectively, and are well known features for \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) ion in \(\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{d}}\) environment,33,34 consistent with the above XRD and XAS results.
Ultrafast OTA spectroscopy was used to investigate the excited state dynamics of ZIF-67 by selectively exciting the two d-d transitions. Figure 3a shows the femtosecond OTA (fs-OTA) spectra of ZIF-67 thin film in the visible region after exciting the lower-lying d-d band using 1000 nm light. Immediately following excitation, the OTA spectrum shows a negative band with triplet maximum in 520-600 nm region and a broad positive feature at \(>605\ \mathrm{nm}\). The negative band strongly resembles the inverted ground state (GS) absorption of \({}^{4}\mathrm{A}_{2}(\mathrm{F})-{}^{4}\mathrm{T}_{1}(\mathrm{P})\) transition, and can thus be attributed to the bleach of \({}^{4}\mathrm{A}_{2}(\mathrm{F})\) state due to photoexcitation. The broad positive band decays within \(\sim200\) ps (inset of Figure 3a), which is accompanied by the emergence of a new positive band centered at 605 nm. These two features are separated by a clear isosbestic point at 647 nm, suggesting that the decay of the former is associated with the formation of the latter. Meanwhile, another positive band was formed at \(\sim525\ \mathrm{nm}\) within the same time regime. All of these spectral features together made the whole OTA spectra derivative-like shape, which maintains through the microsecond time as shown in nanosecond OTA (ns-OTA) spectra (Figure 3b), where the recovery of GS bleach and the decay of the two absorption bands centered 525 nm and 605 nm occur simultaneously. The presence of two isosbestic points at 536 nm and 599 nm confirms that these spectral evolutions are associated with the same recombination process. Similar spectral features were also observed in the near-IR OTA spectra representing the
\({}^{4}\mathrm{A}_{2}(\mathrm{F}){-}^{4}\mathrm{T}_{1}(\mathrm{F})\) transition (Figure S3), consistent with our assignment earlier that the two d-d bands share the same GS, i. e. \({}^{4}\mathrm{A}_{2}(\mathrm{F})\).
Upon excitation of the higher-lying d-d band using 530 nm light, both fs- (Figure 3c) and ns-OTA spectra (Figure 3d) show the similar spectral features as the spectra after 1000 nm excitation, i.e. the instantly formed GS bleach and broad absorption \((>605\ \mathrm{nm})\) which decays to form a long-lived intermediate state with derivative-like spectral feature. However, extra rising components were observed in both GS bleach and \(605\;\mathrm{nm}\) absorption band in the spectra of 530 nm excitation. These additional features can be more clearly seen in Figure 3e, where the kinetic traces at \(580\ \mathrm{nm}\) and \(608\ \mathrm{nm}\) after \(530\ \mathrm{nm}\) and 1000 nm excitation were compared.
Given the interpretation of the OTA spectra above, we can assign the transient species to each specific process according to the model proposed in Figure 2b. The 1000 nm excitation \((h\upsilon_{i})\) depletes \({}^{4}\mathrm{A}_{2}(\mathrm{F})\) state accounting for the GS bleach in 520-600 nm and 900-1000 nm regions, and populates \({}^{4}\mathrm{T}_{1}(\mathrm{F})\) excited state accounting for the instantly formed broad absorption at \(>605\ \mathrm{nm}\). The latter can be assigned to the photoinduced absorption of \({}^{4}\mathrm{T}_{1}(\mathrm{F})\) state, which quickly decays to form the long-lived intermediate state (IMS) featured by the absorption bands centered at \(525\ \mathrm{nm}\) and \(605\ \mathrm{nm}\). The kinetic traces at \(580\ \mathrm{nm}\) and \(608\ \mathrm{nm}\) in fs-OTA spectra can be fitted using bi-exponential functions, from which we obtained a short decay \((580\ \mathrm{nm})\) and rising \((608\ \mathrm{nm})\) component with 31.7 ps and a ultraslow decay component with \(>>5\) ns (Table 1). The 31.7 ps time constant can be assigned to the formation time of the IMS (Figure 2b). The ultraslow decay component can be accurately determined after fitting the ns-OTA kinetics (Figure 3f), from which we obtained two time constants with \(0.64\ \upmu\mathrm{s}\ (74.2\%)\) and \(9.2\ \upmu\mathrm{s}\ (25.8\%)\). The obtained amplitude weighted average time is \(2.9\ \upmu\mathrm{s}\), suggesting the super-long lifetime of this IMS.
The kinetic traces at \(580\ \mathrm{nm}\) and \(608\ \mathrm{nm}\) in fs-OTA spectra after \(530\ \mathrm{nm}\) excitation can be fitted using the same set of time constants above plus an additional rising component in each case (\(\tau_{2}\) in Table 1). Because \(530\ \mathrm{nm}\) and 1000 nm light excite \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) center from the same \({}^{4}\mathrm{A}_{2}(\mathrm{F})\) to two different excited states, i. e. \({}^{4}\mathrm{T}_{1}(\mathrm{F})\) and \({}^{4}\mathrm{T}_{1}(\mathrm{F})\), respectively, we attribute the extra rising component \((0.45\ \mathrm{ps})\) at GS bleach of \(530\ \mathrm{nm}\) excitation spectra to the relaxation process from \({}^{4}\mathrm{T}_{1}(\mathrm{F})\) to \({}^{4}\mathrm{T}_{1}(\mathrm{F})\), while the additional 0.95 ps time constant used in 608 nm kinetics to the formation of IMS directly from \({}^{4}\mathrm{T}_{1}(\mathrm{P})\) state (Figure 2b). The ns-OTA kinetics after \(530\ \mathrm{nm}\) excitation (Figure S4) can be adequately fitted using the same exponential function used in 1000 nm excitation, which further supports the formation of the same long-lived IMS regardless the excitation of different d-d transitions.
It is interesting to note that the OTA spectra of the long-lived IMS closely match the second derivative of the GS ab
sorption band (Figure S5). The derivative-like features have been observed in inorganic- and organic-semiconductors, and are typically attributed to the presence of local fields due to the photoinduced electron-hole separation, which modifies the optical spectra due to the Stark effect.35,36 The derivative-like spectral feature, along with extremely long lifetime of IMS, implies that the final IMS formed in ZIF-67 film after photoexcitation might be a charge separated (CS) state rather than a metal centered state. To test this hypothesis and identify the nature of this IMS, we used XTA, a powerful technique in capturing the intermediate electronic structure at specific metal center,37-39 to probe the IMS electronic structure at Co center.
Figure 4a shows the XANES spectra of ZIF-67 before (laser-off) and after \(527\ \mathrm{nm}\) excitation (laser-on), as well as the difference spectrum (blue curve) obtained after subtracting the laser-off spectrum from the laser-on spectrum. The laser-on spectrum was taken at 500 ps after laser pump pulse when IMS has completely formed according to the OTA results. Therefore, any transient signal observed at this time delay is directly correlated with the IMS electronic structure. As shown in the difference spectrum in Figure 4a, a broad positive transient absorption was observed at white-line region \((7714.9-7725.3\ \mathrm{eV})\) where 1S-4p shake-down \((\mathrm{SD})^{40}\) and 1s-4p transition41 occurs, indicating that the edge of Co center shifts to lower energy and hence the reduction of Co center due to photoexcitation. Because Co atom and imidazolate ligand are the only two components in ZIF-67, the reduction of Co center must be accompanied by the oxidation of the ligand, which unambiguously confirm the formation of CS state with LMCT character. The LMCT nature of the IMS is further supported by the negative feature at \(7700.7\ \mathrm{eV}\), corresponding to the reduced intensity of pre-edge (1s-3d) transition,32,41, where the photoinduced charge separation by LMCT reduces the number of unoccupied Co 3d orbitals and hence the possibility of 1s-3d transition.
In addition to the transient signals at white-line and pre-edge region, noticeable spectral changes were also observed at higher energy region (A and B in Figure 4). Feature A corresponds to multiple scattering processes. Feature B peaked around \(7772\ \mathrm{eV}\) mainly comes from the single-scattering of the outgoing photoelectron from the nearest N atoms. To better illustrate the spectral changes in this region, we generated a new “laser-on” spectrum by adding the difference
spectrum (x 10) to the laser-off spectrum. As shown in Figure 4b, clear red-shift is observed for B band in the "laser-on" spectrum (red plot) compared to the laser-off spectrum (black plot), which indicates the Co-N bond elongation after photoexcitation.
In summary, we have examined the excited state and CS dynamics in ZIF-67 thin film using the combination of ultrafast OTA and XTA spectroscopy. OTA results clearly demonstrate the formation of an exceptionally long-lived IMS (\(\sim\!2.9\) \(\upmu\mathrm{s}\)) within picosecond time scale after the photoexcitation of the dipole-allowed d-d transition of \(C0^{2+}\) ion in ZIF-67. Using powerful element specific XTA spectroscopy, this IMS was unambiguously confirmed to be the CS state with LMCT character. The unusually long-lived CS state, the broad absorption in UV-visible-Near IR region, along with its hybrid porous nature, strongly imply the potential application of ZIF-67 materials in photocatalysis and energy conversion. The future work on understanding the localization/delocalization properties of this long-lived CS state in ZIF-67 framework is essential to facilitate these applications, which is currently undergoing in the group.
